  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "defaultTags": ["tactics"],
  "intro": "Six positions, each one running through a line from an earlier chapter and stopping on the move that matters. Play the line and find the last move yourself.\n\nSeveral of them are defensive, and that is deliberate. In most openings the exercises are about landing a blow. In the Najdorf, half of what a club player needs is seeing the blow coming: White sacrifices on d5, on f5, on e6 and on b5 in these structures, and knowing which ones work is worth more than any attacking idea Black has.\n\n## What each one is about\n\nThe first is the tempo. In the main line of chapter 1, with White's pawn arriving on f5 and everything hanging, there is a capture that comes with check, and a free move in a race decides the game.\n\nThe second is the order of operations. Black's knight is attacked and Black pushes a pawn on the other wing instead, because the pawn comes with a threat of its own and White has to answer first.\n\nThe third is the break. White has spent moves on a rook and a pawn storm and has not castled, and the answer to a wing attack with the king still in the middle is the centre.\n\nThe fourth is defence: a bishop appears on h6 offering to trade off the piece that guards the squares around Black's king, and there is a way to accept on Black's terms rather than White's.\n\nThe fifth is the second look. White finally puts a knight on d5, Black has spent the whole opening preparing to trade it off, and on this particular board there is something better. The prepared answer is still sound; it is just not the best move, and that is worth meeting once.\n\nThe sixth is the outpost. White opens the position and there is one square that is Black's for the rest of the game.\n\n## The pattern that is not here\n\nThe exchange sacrifice, a rook taking a knight on c3, is the signature blow of the Sicilian and it is not one of these six. It is not a move to memorise; it is a judgement about whether the dark squares and the pawn on e4 are worth the exchange, and it comes up in positions too varied to drill. Recognise it as an option whenever White's knight on c3 is the piece holding the position together, and then count.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nAnswering an attack on a piece by moving the piece, when a bigger threat is available. Taking a free pawn without checking whether the capture also comes with check. Defending a wing attack on the wing rather than in the centre. And trading a good bishop for a knight because the trade is there, rather than because the position needs it.",
